  "tutSuppliersTitle": "Suppliers",
  "tutSuppliersSummary": "Hat: purchaser. Create two suppliers and give them a price list for your components.",
  "tutSuppliersNeedsTitle": "Chapter 1 first",
  "tutSuppliersNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the four products from chapter 1 (TUT-MOTOR, TUT-FRAME, TUT-CTRL and TUT-CART) and could not find them. Go back to the chapter map and finish chapter 1 first, then come back here.",
  "tutSuppliersOpenTitle": "Put on the purchaser hat",
  "tutSuppliersOpenBody": "Your components have to come from somewhere. Open Purchasing in the menu: the Suppliers list is the first segment.",
  "tutSuppliersSupplierATitle": "The first supplier",
  "tutSuppliersSupplierABody": "Create a supplier with code TUT-SUP-A and name Voltaic Drives Ltd. Code and name are the only required fields; payment terms, tax id and contact details can follow later.",
  "tutSuppliersSupplierBTitle": "A second supplier",
  "tutSuppliersSupplierBBody": "A purchaser likes to compare. Create a second supplier with code TUT-SUP-B and name Ironbridge Frames.",
  "tutSuppliersOpenATitle": "Open the first supplier",
  "tutSuppliersOpenABody": "Open Voltaic Drives Ltd from the list. Its price list is at the bottom of the supplier screen.",
  "tutSuppliersPriceMotorTitle": "A price for the motor",
  "tutSuppliersPriceMotorBody": "Add TUT-MOTOR to the price list: unit price 120, lead time 14 days, minimum order quantity 5. Purchase orders use these values to pre-fill their lines.",
  "tutSuppliersPriceMotorHint": "Pick the product with the search box in the dialog; type TUT-MOTOR.",
  "tutSuppliersPriceFrameTitle": "A price for the frame",
  "tutSuppliersPriceFrameBody": "Same again for TUT-FRAME: unit price 45, lead time 10 days, minimum order quantity 5.",
  "tutSuppliersPriceControllerTitle": "A price for the controller",
  "tutSuppliersPriceControllerBody": "And TUT-CTRL: unit price 30, lead time 7 days, minimum order quantity 5. This supplier now covers all three components.",
  "tutSuppliersOpenBTitle": "Open the second supplier",
  "tutSuppliersOpenBBody": "Go back to the list and open Ironbridge Frames.",
  "tutSuppliersPriceMotorBTitle": "A competing price",
  "tutSuppliersPriceMotorBBody": "Give this supplier a price for TUT-MOTOR too: unit price 135, lead time 5 days, minimum order quantity 1. It is dearer but faster; that is the kind of choice a price list helps with.",
  "tutSuppliersDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutSuppliersDoneBody": "A supplier is a record with payment terms and contacts, and its price list links it to products with a price, a lead time and a minimum order quantity. Next: ordering the goods.",
  "tutBuyingTitle": "Buying and receiving",
  "tutBuyingSummary": "Hats: purchaser and goods-in. Order the components, send the order and receive the goods in two deliveries.",
  "tutBuyingNeedsTitle": "Chapters 1 and 2 first",
  "tutBuyingNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the products of chapter 1 and the supplier Voltaic Drives Ltd with its price list from chapter 2, and could not find the products. Finish those chapters first.",
  "tutBuyingOpenTitle": "The orders list",
  "tutBuyingOpenBody": "Open Purchasing. Next to Suppliers there is a segment for purchase orders; open it.",
  "tutBuyingOrderTitle": "Order the components",
  "tutBuyingOrderBody": "Create a purchase order for Voltaic Drives Ltd. After choosing the supplier its goods are listed: tick TUT-MOTOR, TUT-FRAME and TUT-CTRL and set the quantity to 30 for each, then save. The prices come from the price list.",
  "tutBuyingOrderHint": "Choose the supplier first. Ticking a box fills in the minimum order quantity (or 1); type 30 in the quantity box instead. Use the filter box to find a product quickly.",
  "tutBuyingOpenOrderTitle": "The order screen",
  "tutBuyingOpenOrderBody": "Open the order you just made. A purchase order is a draft first; the buttons at the top follow its life: approve, send, receive.",
  "tutBuyingApproveTitle": "Approve the order",
  "tutBuyingApproveBody": "Approving is a separate permission, so a purchaser can prepare orders that a manager releases. As admin you wear both hats: press Approve.",
  "tutBuyingSendTitle": "Send it to the supplier",
  "tutBuyingSendBody": "Press Send. The order is marked as sent and the supplier gets it. In test and tutorial environments outgoing mail is only written to files on the server, nothing leaves the building.",
  "tutBuyingOpenWarehousesTitle": "Where do the goods go?",
  "tutBuyingOpenWarehousesBody": "Goods need a place before they can be received. Open Stock and press Warehouses: this is where warehouses and their locations are kept.",
  "tutBuyingWarehouseTitle": "Create the warehouse",
  "tutBuyingWarehouseBody": "Press New warehouse, give it the code MAIN and the name Main warehouse, and save. A new warehouse comes with its location MAIN, which is where you receive the goods next. If saving is refused because the code MAIN is taken, an inactive warehouse holds it: switch it (and its location) back on in Stock > Warehouses, or give it another code with its edit button, then press Next.",
  "tutBuyingWarehouseHint": "Code and name are both required. If a warehouse with a location exists already, this step is skipped.",
  "tutBuyingReceivePartTitle": "The first delivery arrives",
  "tutBuyingReceivePartBody": "Put on the goods-in hat. Press Receive goods, choose the warehouse location MAIN and book 10 pieces of each component, fewer than ordered. The order becomes partially received.",
  "tutBuyingReceivePartHint": "Change the quantity of each line in the dialog; the remaining quantity is shown next to it. A delivery note number is optional.",
  "tutBuyingReceiveRestTitle": "The rest arrives",
  "tutBuyingReceiveRestBody": "Receive the remaining 20 pieces of each component the same way. When everything is in, the order is fully received; each delivery keeps its own goods receipt.",
  "tutBuyingStockTitle": "Look at the stock",
  "tutBuyingStockBody": "Open Stock. The 30 motors, frames and controllers are on hand in warehouse MAIN. Every receipt created a stock movement, so the quantity can always be traced back.",
  "tutBuyingDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutBuyingDoneBody": "A purchase order goes draft, approved, sent, received. Goods can arrive in several deliveries and every delivery adds to stock. The supplier's invoice comes next.",
  "tutInvoicesTitle": "Checking the invoices",
  "tutInvoicesSummary": "Hat: accountant. Register supplier invoices, read the three-way match, approve, dispute and pay.",
  "tutInvoicesNeedsTitle": "Chapter 3 first",
  "tutInvoicesNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the purchase order from chapter 3, fully received, and it is not (yet). Finish chapter 3 first.",
  "tutInvoicesOpenOrderTitle": "Back to the order",
  "tutInvoicesOpenOrderBody": "The supplier's invoice arrives. Open the purchase order from chapter 3 again: once goods are received it offers a shortcut to register the invoice.",
  "tutInvoicesCreateTitle": "Register the invoice",
  "tutInvoicesCreateBody": "Press Create supplier invoice. Type the supplier's own invoice number, for example TUT-INV-001, and save. The lines are filled in from what was ordered and received, so there is nothing to type.",
  "tutInvoicesCreateHint": "The invoice date and due date default to today and to the payment terms of the supplier.",
  "tutInvoicesMatchTitle": "The three-way match",
  "tutInvoicesMatchBody": "The banner compares three documents: what was ordered, what was received and what is invoiced, line by line. Green means they agree within the tolerances; the tolerances are shown in the banner. Press Next when you have looked.",
  "tutInvoicesApproveTitle": "Approve the invoice",
  "tutInvoicesApproveBody": "The match is green, so press Approve. An approved invoice is ready for payment.",
  "tutInvoicesSecondTitle": "A suspicious invoice",
  "tutInvoicesSecondBody": "Now a second invoice arrives from Voltaic Drives Ltd, this time without a purchase order: in Finance, create a supplier invoice for that supplier, number TUT-INV-002, leave the order empty and add one line with description Motors, quantity 10, unit price 150.",
  "tutInvoicesSecondHint": "The real motor price is 120 and this line has no order line to compare with. The app cannot change prices of an order-based invoice, so a free-text invoice is how a mismatch shows up here.",
  "tutInvoicesMismatchTitle": "The match fails",
  "tutInvoicesMismatchBody": "The banner is orange: a free-text line has nothing it can be matched against, so it cannot be checked against an order or a receipt. The line shows why. Press Next when you have looked.",
  "tutInvoicesDisputeTitle": "Dispute the invoice",
  "tutInvoicesDisputeBody": "Press Dispute and give a reason, for example price does not match the agreement. A disputed invoice cannot be paid until it is reopened. (An approver can also approve a failed match with an override reason, which is kept on the invoice.)",
  "tutInvoicesTolerancesTitle": "The tolerances",
  "tutInvoicesTolerancesBody": "How far an invoice may differ is a setting: open the Settings segment of Finance to see the quantity and price tolerances in percent. Look, then press Next; changing them is up to you.",
  "tutInvoicesPayTitle": "Pay the good invoice",
  "tutInvoicesPayBody": "Open the first supplier invoice you registered (the one that matched and was approved) and press Mark as paid. Enter a payment reference such as TUT-PAY-001. The app records the payment; the bank transfer itself happens elsewhere.",
  "tutInvoicesDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutInvoicesDoneBody": "The three-way match compares order, receipt and invoice before money moves. Matching invoices are approved and paid, doubtful ones are disputed. Next: building the carts yourself.",
  "tutManufacturingTitle": "Manufacturing",
  "tutManufacturingSummary": "Hats: production manager and production worker. Set up a division, then build five carts from the components.",
  "tutManufacturingNeedsTitle": "Chapters 1 to 3 first",
  "tutManufacturingNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the cart with its bill of materials (chapter 1) and components in stock (chapter 3), and could not find the products. Finish those chapters first.",
  "tutManufacturingOpenDivisionsTitle": "Divisions",
  "tutManufacturingOpenDivisionsBody": "Production work is organised in divisions, for example an assembly line. Only an administrator defines them. Open Production and use the gear button at the top right to reach the divisions.",
  "tutManufacturingDivisionTitle": "Create a division",
  "tutManufacturingDivisionBody": "Add a division with code TUT-ASM and name Tutorial assembly, and tick yourself in the member list: members are the workers of the division.",
  "tutManufacturingDivisionHint": "The dialog lists the users of the company; tick the chip with your own name.",
  "tutManufacturingQueueTitle": "The production queue",
  "tutManufacturingQueueBody": "Go back to Production. In the queue header choose My divisions: it shows the orders of your divisions plus orders without a division, so nothing unassigned is hidden. You are a member of a division, so this is the default for you; choose All divisions to see everything.",
  "tutManufacturingReleaseTitle": "Release a production order",
  "tutManufacturingReleaseBody": "Press Build for stock, choose TUT-CART and quantity 5, and release. The bill of materials is frozen and the components are reserved. Normally production starts from a sales order line; you will see that in chapter 9.",
  "tutManufacturingReleaseHint": "Only manufactured products with an active bill of materials can be released.",
  "tutManufacturingAssignTitle": "Give it to the division",
  "tutManufacturingAssignBody": "The new order is open. Choose the division Tutorial assembly in the Division field and press Save, so that the order shows up in the queue of that division.",
  "tutManufacturingStartTitle": "Start the work",
  "tutManufacturingStartBody": "Put on the worker hat and press Start. The order moves from released to in progress.",
  "tutManufacturingWarehouseTitle": "A place for the carts",
  "tutManufacturingWarehouseBody": "The finished carts need a location to go into, and there is no warehouse yet (chapter 3 creates it). Open Stock, press Warehouses and create the warehouse MAIN with the name Main warehouse, then go back to the production order. If saving is refused because the code MAIN is taken, an inactive warehouse holds it: switch it (and its location) back on in Stock > Warehouses, or give it another code with its edit button, then press Next.",
  "tutManufacturingWarehouseHint": "The location MAIN is created together with the warehouse. If a warehouse with a location exists already, this step is skipped.",
  "tutManufacturingCompleteTitle": "Complete the order",
  "tutManufacturingCompleteBody": "Press Complete. The dialog shows the output quantity (5) and the components consumed. Choose the warehouse location MAIN in Put into, so the finished carts have a place to go, and confirm: the components leave the stock and five finished carts enter it.",
  "tutManufacturingCompleteHint": "Leave the planned values as they are; the dialog lets you record scrap or extra consumption when reality differs.",
  "tutManufacturingStockTitle": "Check the stock",
  "tutManufacturingStockBody": "Open Stock: TUT-CART now has 5 on hand, and each component is down by 5 (25 left of each). Production is what links purchasing to selling.",
  "tutManufacturingDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutManufacturingDoneBody": "Divisions group production workers; a production order freezes the bill of materials, reserves components, is started and completed, and turns components into finished goods. Next: prices.",
  "tutPricesTitle": "Prices and zones",
  "tutPricesSummary": "Hat: sales manager. Create a price zone and give the cart a price in two zones.",
  "tutPricesNeedsTitle": "Chapter 1 first",
  "tutPricesNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the cart from chapter 1 and could not find it. Finish chapter 1 first.",
  "tutPricesOpenAdminTitle": "Open the admin area",
  "tutPricesOpenAdminBody": "A price depends on the customer's zone: a country or market with its own price list. Zones are managed under Admin. Open Admin and choose the Price zones segment; the company already has zones Z1 to Z5.",
  "tutPricesZoneTitle": "Create a zone",
  "tutPricesZoneBody": "Add a price zone with code TUT-EXPORT and name Export zone. Customers are placed in a zone later, when you create one.",
  "tutPricesZoneHint": "Choose the Price zones segment at the top of the Admin page first.",
  "tutPricesOpenCartTitle": "Open the cart",
  "tutPricesOpenCartBody": "Open the product TUT-CART from the Products list. Its first tab, Prices, holds the prices per zone.",
  "tutPricesPriceExportTitle": "A price in the new zone",
  "tutPricesPriceExportBody": "Press New price, choose zone TUT-EXPORT, enter unit price 1450 (excluding VAT) and save.",
  "tutPricesPriceExportHint": "The valid-from date defaults to today; a later date schedules a price change.",
  "tutPricesPriceHomeTitle": "A price in another zone",
  "tutPricesPriceHomeBody": "Add a second price for the cart in one of the existing zones, for example Z1 at 1200. The same product can now cost different amounts depending on the customer's zone.",
  "tutPricesDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutPricesDoneBody": "A price zone groups customers that get the same price list, and a product has one price per zone and date. Quotations pick the price of the customer's zone. Next: a new customer writes in.",
  "tutCustomerTitle": "A new customer writes in",
  "tutCustomerSummary": "Hats: sales and helpdesk. A customer e-mail becomes a ticket; the sender is new, so you ask for company details and create the customer.",
  "tutCustomerNeedsTitle": "Chapter 6 first",
  "tutCustomerNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the price zone TUT-EXPORT from chapter 6 (the new customer gets it) and could not find it. Finish the earlier chapters first.",
  "tutCustomerMail1Title": "An e-mail arrives",
  "tutCustomerMail1Body": "Someone you do not know asks for a quotation for your cart. A real mailbox would deliver the message by itself; here the button below plays the customer and hands a ready-made e-mail (from Maya Okafor, example.test) to the helpdesk, exactly as the mail importer does.",
  "tutCustomerMail1Action": "Simulate customer e-mail",
  "tutCustomerOpenHelpdeskTitle": "The ticket",
  "tutCustomerOpenHelpdeskBody": "Every incoming message becomes a ticket. Open Helpdesk: the ticket from Maya Okafor is in the list.",
  "tutCustomerOpenTicketTitle": "Open the ticket",
  "tutCustomerOpenTicketBody": "Open the ticket. The conversation is on the left; on the right are its properties. No customer is linked yet, because nobody with this address exists in the system.",
  "tutCustomerReplyTitle": "Ask for company details",
  "tutCustomerReplyBody": "Reply to Maya: thank her and ask for the company name, VAT number and address, then press Send. The reply is queued for the mail worker. In test and tutorial environments outgoing mail is only written to files on the server, so Maya never receives it.",
  "tutCustomerReplyHint": "Make sure the Reply tab (not Internal note) is selected above the message box.",
  "tutCustomerMail2Title": "The customer answers",
  "tutCustomerMail2Body": "Use the button to play Maya's answer. It is a reply to her first message, so it lands in the same ticket as a new message, with her company name, VAT number and address.",
  "tutCustomerMail2Action": "Simulate customer e-mail",
  "tutCustomerReadDetailsTitle": "Read the details",
  "tutCustomerReadDetailsBody": "Go back to the ticket (reopen it if it does not show the new message yet) and read Maya's answer: company Brightwheel Logistics BV, VAT number BE0123456749, Voorbeeldlaan 12, 9000 Gent, Belgium. Press Next when you have it.",
  "tutCustomerCreateCustomerTitle": "Create the customer",
  "tutCustomerCreateCustomerBody": "In the ticket properties press New customer. Fill in name Brightwheel Logistics BV, a code such as TUT-CUST, VAT number, the address, and choose the price zone TUT-EXPORT. The ticket is linked to the new customer when you save.",
  "tutCustomerCreateCustomerHint": "Street Voorbeeldlaan 12, postal code 9000, city Gent, country BE. Language and payment terms can stay as they are.",
  "tutCustomerLinkedTitle": "The ticket knows its customer",
  "tutCustomerLinkedBody": "The ticket now shows the customer, and new buttons appear: create a lead or start a quotation straight from the ticket. The next chapter makes the quotation. Press Next.",
  "tutCustomerDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutCustomerDoneBody": "Incoming mail becomes tickets, a reply asks the customer for what is missing, and a customer can be created from the ticket so that everything stays linked. Next: the quotation.",
  "tutQuotationTitle": "The quotation",
  "tutQuotationSummary": "Hat: salesperson. Quote 8 carts to the new customer, get an answer from production on timing and send it.",
  "tutQuotationNeedsTitle": "Chapter 7 first",
  "tutQuotationNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the customer Brightwheel Logistics BV from chapter 7 and could not find it. Finish chapter 7 first.",
  "tutQuotationOpenQuotationsTitle": "Quotations",
  "tutQuotationOpenQuotationsBody": "Open Quotations in the menu. This list holds every quotation with its number, customer and status.",
  "tutQuotationCreateTitle": "Write the quotation",
  "tutQuotationCreateBody": "Press New quotation, choose Brightwheel Logistics BV, add TUT-CART with quantity 8 and save the draft. The unit price comes from the customer's price zone, TUT-EXPORT, so it is the 1450 you entered in chapter 6.",
  "tutQuotationCreateHint": "Use the product search in the lines section; type TUT-CART. If the customer is not preselected, search for Brightwheel.",
  "tutQuotationLeadTitle": "How long will it take?",
  "tutQuotationLeadBody": "The customer asked for a delivery time. Honestly: the app does not calculate lead times automatically yet, and a quotation has no delivery date field. So ask production (put on that hat, look at the production queue and the components in stock) and write the answer into the quotation's notes, for example: Delivery about 3 weeks after order. Save the draft.",
  "tutQuotationLeadHint": "On an order you can send production a question as a ticket (you will see that in chapter 9); quotations do not have that button yet.",
  "tutQuotationSendTitle": "Send the quotation",
  "tutQuotationSendBody": "Press Mark as sent. From now on the quotation is frozen: it can only be accepted, rejected or revised. (Mail is not sent from this button; you would send the PDF yourself or reply in the ticket.)",
  "tutQuotationPdfTitle": "The PDF",
  "tutQuotationPdfBody": "The PDF button shows the quotation as the customer would see it. It is made on the server with a typesetting tool; on a server without it you get an error message, which does not affect anything else. Look if you like, then press Next.",
  "tutQuotationPdfHint": "Nothing breaks if the PDF fails: the quotation is already sent.",
  "tutQuotationDoneTitle": "What you learned",
  "tutQuotationDoneBody": "A quotation takes the price of the customer's zone, carries notes and freezes when it is sent. Delivery times are still your judgement call, together with production. Next: the customer wants a change.",
  "tutChangeTitle": "Change, order, production",
  "tutChangeSummary": "Hats: sales and production. Revise the quotation, turn it into an order, build the carts and a few extra for stock.",
  "tutChangeNeedsTitle": "Chapter 8 first",
  "tutChangeNeedsBody": "This chapter needs the sent quotation from chapter 8 and could not find it. Finish chapter 8 first.",
  "tutChangeMail3Title": "The customer answers again",
  "tutChangeMail3Body": "Maya is happy with the quotation but wants 10 carts instead of 8. Use the button to play her e-mail; it joins the same ticket.",
  "tutChangeMail3Action": "Simulate customer e-mail",
  "tutChangeOpenQuoteTitle": "Open the sent quotation",
  "tutChangeOpenQuoteBody": "A sent quotation is frozen, so a change means a new revision. Open the quotation from chapter 8.",
  "tutChangeReviseTitle": "Make a revision",
  "tutChangeReviseBody": "Press New revision and choose Keep agreed prices. The old revision is marked superseded and a new draft with the same number opens.",
  "tutChangeReviseHint": "Update to current prices would reprice at today's price list; here the customer keeps the agreed price.",
  "tutChangeEditTitle": "Change the quantity",
  "tutChangeEditBody": "Set the quantity of TUT-CART to 10 and save the draft.",
  "tutChangeEditHint": "The notes of the first revision were copied; adjust them if the delivery time changes.",
  "tutChangeSendTitle": "Send the revision",
  "tutChangeSendBody": "Mark the new revision as sent, as before.",
  "tutChangeAcceptTitle": "The customer accepts",
  "tutChangeAcceptBody": "Maya agrees with the new revision: press Accepted to record it.",
  "tutChangeConvertTitle": "Turn it into a sales order",
  "tutChangeConvertBody": "Press Convert to order. A draft sales order is created from the accepted revision and opens.",
  "tutChangeConvertHint": "The dialog asks for the customer's own reference; type for instance TUT-PO-1 or leave it empty.",
  "tutChangeOpenOrderTitle": "The sales order",
  "tutChangeOpenOrderBody": "If the order did not open by itself, open it from Orders. It has the 10 carts, the price and the customer reference. Orders go from draft to sent to confirmed.",
  "tutChangeSendOrderTitle": "Send the order confirmation",
  "tutChangeSendOrderBody": "Press Send order confirmation. The order becomes sent: the customer can now confirm it.",
  "tutChangeSendOrderHint": "Making the confirmation document needs the server's typesetting tool; if it is missing the server refuses this step and a server with the tool is needed to continue.",
  "tutChangeConfirmTitle": "Confirm on the customer's behalf",
  "tutChangeConfirmBody": "Maya already said yes by e-mail, so press Confirm on behalf and add a note such as confirmed by e-mail. The order is now confirmed and can go to production.",
  "tutChangeReleaseTitle": "To release",
  "tutChangeReleaseBody": "Put on the production hat. Open Production > To release: the confirmed order line with 10 carts is waiting. Press Release to production to create the production order for exactly that line.",
  "tutChangeStartTitle": "Start production",
  "tutChangeStartBody": "Press Start on the new production order.",
  "tutChangeWarehouseTitle": "A place for the carts",
  "tutChangeWarehouseBody": "The finished carts need a location to go into, and there is no warehouse yet (chapter 3 creates it). Open Stock, press Warehouses and create the warehouse MAIN with the name Main warehouse, then go back to the production order. If saving is refused because the code MAIN is taken, an inactive warehouse holds it: switch it (and its location) back on in Stock > Warehouses, or give it another code with its edit button, then press Next.",
  "tutChangeWarehouseHint": "The location MAIN is created together with the warehouse. If a warehouse with a location exists already, this step is skipped.",
  "tutChangeCompleteTitle": "Complete production",
  "tutChangeCompleteBody": "Press Complete, choose the warehouse location MAIN in Put into and confirm the 10 carts. The components are consumed and the carts go to the order.",
  "tutChangeReadyTitle": "Ready for shipping",
  "tutChangeReadyBody": "Open the order again: with all its lines produced it is ready for shipping. Delivery (picking, shipping, invoicing the customer) is outside this tutorial. Press Next.",
  "tutChangeStockTitle": "A few extra for stock",
  "tutChangeStockBody": "Production is often ahead of orders. Open Production, press Build for stock and release 4 more carts of TUT-CART. Without an order they simply land in stock when completed.",
  "tutChangeStockHint": "You can start and complete that order as in chapter 5, or leave it released.",
  "tutChangeDoneTitle": "Well done: the whole story",
  "tutChangeDoneBody": "You went from suppliers to purchasing, invoices, production, prices, a ticket, a new customer, a revised quotation, an order and production for the order and for stock. Where next: deliveries and shipping, repairs, field service and the reports each have their own menu entry. Any chapter can be replayed from the chapter map; steps that are already done are skipped.",
  "tutInvoicesOpenSecondTitle": "Open the suspicious invoice",
  "tutInvoicesOpenSecondBody": "Open invoice TUT-INV-002, the one you just registered, from the list in Finance.",
